<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Imperial Abbey of Lindau</h1><p class="st-meta">1466 CE – 1803 CE (approx.) · capital Lindau</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A house of noble canonesses on an island in Lake Constance whose princess-abbesses ruled a tiny territory beside the free city of Lindau.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The women's community on the island of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-lindau">Lindau</a> was founded in the 9th century, and the town that grew up beside it became a free imperial city. The abbey's direct dependence on the empire was confirmed in the 15th century, and its abbess ranked as a princess of the empire.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The canonesses were noblewomen who lived without permanent vows. The princess-abbess governed scattered villages and rights around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a>, but the abbey was entangled in long disputes with the city of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-lindau">Lindau</a>, which adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in the 1520s, while the abbey remained Catholic.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1802 and 1803 the abbey was secularised and granted to the princes of Bretzenheim, who soon exchanged it; it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and in 1805 to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria</a>. The former abbey buildings stand in Lindau's old town.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/imperial-abbey-of-lindau.jpg" alt="Imperial Abbey of Lindau" width="320" height="212"><figcaption>Image: Matthias Zirngibl from Germany · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lindau_Stiftskirche_und_St_Stephan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1466 CE – 1803 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lindau</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swabian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lindau Abbey</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1634&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-lindau" aria-label="Imperial Abbey of Lindau on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4906 511 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Imperial Abbey of Lindau lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5226 767L5229 694"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5226 767h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5226 767h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1634&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-lindau">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

